{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790966780,"nonce":"BKHoT0DMCtyhDhDz00kgcsfRAVU=","title":"Android (blinc_platform_android + android-activity NativeActivity): resume 'goes black +","message":"Diagnosed on a Pixel 10 Pro (Android 16) running `mobile/example` (`com.blinc.example`) via `adb` at `~/Library/Android/sdk/platform-tools/adb`. Build: `mobile/example/build-android.sh` (cargo-ndk arm64-v8a release -\u003e gradle). Blinc renders to a native surface via wgpu, bypassing HWUI, so `dumpsys gfxinfo` shows 0 frames; measure fps from the app's own `REDRAW #N` log or instrument acquire/render/present with `web_time::Instant`.\n\n**Resume \"shows UI, goes black, re-renders with FRESH state\" = a CRASH + cold restart, NOT a UI rebuild.** The pid changes across resume (`Start proc ... com.blinc.example`), and logcat shows `Abort message: 'assertion failed: previous.is_none()'` -\u003e SIGABRT. That assert is `ndk-context/src/lib.rs:87` (`initialize_android_context`, once-only). The launcher-icon resume spun up a SECOND `MainActivity` instance while the first's `android_main` was still alive, double-initializing the global Android context. Fixes in the app manifest (per-app, downstream apps need it too): `android:launchMode=\"singleTask\"` (icon tap resumes the single instance) AND broaden `android:configChanges` to include uiMode|density|colorMode|fontScale|smallestScreenSize|locale|layoutDirection|... (so config changes don't destroy+recreate the activity). Diagnostic tip: `adb am start -n` does NOT reproduce it; the launcher intent (`monkey -c android.intent.category.LAUNCHER`) or a real icon tap does. Verify the fix by checking `pidof` stays constant across resume cycles.\n\n**Touch scroll (blinc_app/src/android.rs + blinc_layout/src/widgets/scroll.rs):**\n- \"Scroll slow / slower than desktop\": the MOVE handler stored only the LAST `scroll_info` per frame, but `poll_events` drains ALL queued MotionEvents each loop (~120-240Hz touch vs ~35fps loop = 3-7 moves/frame). Accumulate the deltas per frame so the drag is 1:1 and velocity is real.\n- \"Second swipe has no momentum\" (every OTHER flick): `in_momentum_mode` in scroll.rs is a macOS-only flag (macOS delivers scroll-end TWICE: finger-lift + OS momentum-end). Touch fires once, so it latched true and the next flick's `on_scroll_end` early-returned. Clear it at gesture start (in `apply_touch_scroll_delta`'s first-event branch).\n- Grab-to-stop must be TAP-ONLY (desktop calls `cancel_scroll_animation_in_chain` on pointer-down; Android didn't). A swipe must carry/accumulate momentum; only a no-move release stops a coast. Classify tap vs swipe by move-count + total displacement, NOT a per-move threshold (a short/fast flick trips no per-move threshold).\n\n**Compositor fast-path is the WRONG tool on Android (and froze the app).** Routing android.rs through `render_tree_with_motion_opt(Some(surface_texture), try_fast_paint=true)` needs `COPY_DST` on the surface (Android had only `RENDER_ATTACHMENT`) or it aborts with a wgpu validation error. Even fixed, it FROZE the UI (120fps blitting a stale cached layer) because Android never wires the `invalidate_render_cache_tagged` calls desktop makes on scroll/input/state/resize. And it wouldn't fix scroll anyway: scroll reveals new content -\u003e invalidates the cache -\u003e full re-render. Reverted.\n\n**Android scroll fps is RENDER-bound, not pacing/forced-fps.** Instrumented: acquire ~0.1ms, present ~0.4ms, render 14-24ms (the full `render_tree_with_motion` CPU walker re-collecting the whole tree every frame). An FpsAdapter/Choreographer would only cap/pace, not raise it. Real fix = damage/dirty-tracking (deferred).\n\n**First-launch black screen = ~18 Vulkan pipelines compiled serially in `create_pipelines` (~925ms on Tensor G5/PowerVR), NOT device init/fonts/buffers.** Diagnosed by on-device `web_time::Instant` probes (device ~30ms, fonts ~34ms, buffers 0ms). Fixed with a persistent `wgpu::PipelineCache` (commit a5a344ad): `RendererConfig.pipeline_cache_path` -\u003e request `Features::PIPELINE_CACHE`, `device.create_pipeline_cache` with `fallback:true` seeded from disk bytes, pass `Some(\u0026cache)` to every core `RenderPipelineDescriptor.cache`, write `cache.get_data()` back after build. Android threads `AndroidApp::internal_data_path()/blinc_pipeline_cache.bin` (compute it BEFORE the `poll_events` loop; `app` is mutably borrowed inside). Cache file has a validated 40-byte header (magic `BLPC`, version, adapter vendor+device, driver-string hash, payload hash) so a driver/GPU swap fails validation and cold-builds instead of feeding stale blobs. Measured 925ms -\u003e 31ms for `create_pipelines` on 2nd+ launch (loads a ~254KB blob); driver-agnostic so it helps the Mali/MT6789 reporter too. First-EVER launch (cold install, no cache) still pays the compile; SDF-pair `thread::scope` parallelization trims it modestly on PowerVR (partial driver parallelism). Keep the `tracing::info!(\"Pipeline cache: loaded/cold/saving N bytes\")` logs for verification. Logcat tag is `Blinc` (not `RustStdoutStderr`). Gradle needs JDK 11+; use `JAVA_HOME=/Applications/Android Studio.app/Contents/jbr/Contents/Home`. NDK pinned r29 (`$ANDROID_HOME/ndk/29.0.14206865`).","files":null}]}